investigations.fix_sketch:
A: move the early-out in stroke_path_internal after line_width and reach are computed, and test the bounds widened by the outline's reach plus half a fringe. This is the patch I tested, stroke-cull-by-outline.patch:
  let reach = stroke.reach(line_width);
  let cull = reach + self.fringe_width * 0.5;
  if bounds.maxx + cull < 0.0 || bounds.minx - cull > width || bounds.maxy + cull < 0.0 || bounds.miny - cull > height { return; }
It mirrors Skia's quickReject with computeFastBounds and GetInflationRadius. It is bit-identical on every other failing (file, zoom) pair, and it adds no per-draw work beyond reordering arithmetic. The fill early-out (lib.rs:1333-1340) is right as it is: a fill's outline is its path.

B: neither blend rule gives the union.
- The stencil first-wins proof (Equal 0 with IncrementClamp in the AA pass) gets coincident ends right but halves abutting ones (the seam gets worse, 75 % to 50 %), and the result depends on contour order.
- A MAX blend of fringe coverage has the same split: right for coincident ends, wrong for abutting ones.
- Adding the cell areas (clip/coverage.rs) over-inks coincident ends to 100 %.
The browsers resolve winding per sample: Skia's supersampling scan converter resolves nonzero on each sub-scanline with exact span ends; analytic AA does the same where it applies. A real fix would draw a stroke that has two or more contours whose reach-widened bounds intersect through a coverage mask (#408's atlas) that resolves nonzero per sub-scanline (4 sub-rows with exact horizontal coverage, as SkScan_AntiPath does), applied only where the fringes meet. That gate keeps single-contour strokes, the common case, free. It also shrinks the seams in #417's dash pieces. The visible impact is small (26-38 px at 64/255 here), so it should be filed but ranks low.

